WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.840
You might need to click as accept to being a panel, or host.

2
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.430
Do you want to kick off for us, Kevin, because we've only got 15 minutes for this?

3
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.581
Yeah. Hold on a second. I'll just have to...

4
00:00:23.715 --> 00:00:25.710
Brent was going to do the intro, so hold on a second.

5
00:00:25.741 --> 00:00:26.040
Let me-

6
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:27.930
Emma's on now.

7
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.030
And,

8
00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:31.440
Brent.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.550
It's me.

10
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.911
Hear you, Brent. Just can't see you, but that's okay.

11
00:00:36.481 --> 00:00:36.885
Unless you've got-

12
00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.050
I think you're better off that way, Chair.

13
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.390
Are you wearing a nice bow tie this morning?

14
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.810
On this occasion, I'm not, and that's better for you.

15
00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.390
So I just don't have my camera. Why is my camera not on?

16
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:55.380
So-

17
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:56.401
Come.

18
00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:03.090
All right. So thank you. See, I need this assistance, and you

19
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.485
guys are far more

20
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.360
skilled than I am in this regard. So can you see me now?

21
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.620
Yes, we can. Yep. Wave your hands if you want to open with a few points.

22
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.400
Okay. Let me just kick on, if I may. So, thank you, Chair, and members of the

23
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.170
committee. We really appreciate the opportunity to come along and talk to you

24
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:30.690
today. So on behalf of the Non-Bank Deposit Takers Association, thank you for the

25
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.350
opportunity. We'd like to focus on two factors today.

26
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.000
First, proportionality, and the second one is on capital.

27
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.940
So NBDTs have really strong regulation, our

28
00:01:45.391 --> 00:01:49.980
prudential supervision, and a safe and stable system.

29
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:56.130
However, it remains important that the regulatory requirements reflect the size and

30
00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.771
the scale of the institutions. This is really important. It does relate to us.

31
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.540
All of the banks are larger than us, so they have a bigger asset base over which to

32
00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:11.430
spread the costs. As we're going into this process, there's no question that the

33
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:17.430
costs are escalating, and that's putting pressure upon all the new players in the

34
00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.770
Non-Bank Deposit Taker Association.

35
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.135
So please, if you could, focus on proportionality.

36
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.555
Remember that things such as bank levy, doesn't seem like a lot of money, but it is

37
00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:35.535
proportionate. It comes to us, it hurts, and it just adds to our

38
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.370
costs. And you know, it's like inflation.

39
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.530
We know that the costs will go up, it will creep, and very seldom will it go away.

40
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:49.320
So we want to really focus on any of those sort of, they seem insignificant,

41
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.070
but they are very significant across a smaller asset base.

42
00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.530
General Capital is the parent company of my business. I'm the CEO of that.

43
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:04.171
We're listed on NZX. We have costs, and we have the ability to raise additional

44
00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:09.450
capital. General Finance is the licensed body. They are the subsidiary.

45
00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:12.690
And so we've got two levels of cost within that structure.

46
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.710
I do want to reinforce the fact that

47
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:23.460
if we do not make money, then the smaller end of the businesses will fail.

48
00:03:23.851 --> 00:03:27.300
And we've seen what happens out of that. We've seen the bigger banks buy you.

49
00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.250
And if you look at someone like Heartland Bank, which has three NBDTs in it,

50
00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:38.580
virtually every bank around has got an NBDT in it, right from ANZ,

51
00:03:38.671 --> 00:03:42.570
right down. So if you really want competition, if that is a

52
00:03:44.041 --> 00:03:49.380
motivator in here, if we do want to get competition in the sector, we have to make

53
00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:55.110
sure the costs are proportional and that we are able to gain

54
00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.690
returns on our capital. If I look at the smaller banks, the return on the banks

55
00:04:00.781 --> 00:04:05.190
like Bank of Baroda and Bank of India, et cetera, their returns are very low.

56
00:04:05.791 --> 00:04:10.440
So, they would not be around if they did not have parents back in India.

57
00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.860
So if you are serious, you do have to help us by controlling the

58
00:04:16.921 --> 00:04:22.380
costs and by assisting us through that next level of control of costs.

59
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.545
We all know that the market is relatively concentrated here.

60
00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.870
But if you look at the costs, if we all want to go and set up new computer systems,

61
00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:34.335
the

62
00:04:35.701 --> 00:04:37.650
amount of cost is absolutely massive.

63
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.890
I really implore you as a team watching over the Reserve Bank, that you are

64
00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:49.890
thinking about cost as a major, major issue if you do expect this to

65
00:04:49.951 --> 00:04:52.050
be successful over the next three or four years.

66
00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.670
I'll pass now over to Kevin Hughes. Kevin was with us earlier and thank you.

67
00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.910
Sorry to muck you around earlier, Kevin. I pass to you.

68
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.720
No, thank you, Brent. And, yeah, so hello, I'm Kevin Hughes.

69
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:11.310
I'm the CEO at Unity Credit Union. So we're a small credit union, sort of

70
00:05:11.341 --> 00:05:14.341
headquartered out of Hawke's Bay, got about 35,000 members.

71
00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.485
Following on from what Brent said, just want to highlight around the whole payments

72
00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.690
infrastructure and open banking area.

73
00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:27.045
So, with competition today, it depends not only on the ability to take on deposits

74
00:05:27.045 --> 00:05:31.515
and provide loans, but obviously, also participate in the infrastructure that

75
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:37.230
enables customers to make payments and access modern financial services in a

76
00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:40.619
secure, safe way. And that's really important.

77
00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:45.510
So for smaller organizations like us, the access to that infrastructure is

78
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:50.010
technically feasible, but often not practical or commercially viable.

79
00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.225
And I'd just like to highlight a couple of key examples that are recent examples of

80
00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.890
this around how embedded pricing models that are traditional make it very

81
00:05:58.951 --> 00:06:03.555
challenging for smaller organizations to get engaged in the payments and

82
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.130
transaction banking infrastructure.

83
00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:10.230
And those traditional models are usually based on a fixed rate price and then a

84
00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.010
variable on top. So I'll provide two recent examples. ESAS.

85
00:06:14.341 --> 00:06:19.230
So we're really pleased that the Reserve Bank have made ESAS available to our

86
00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:23.130
sector, and that's been certainly, part of that's been the influence of this

87
00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:23.520
committee.

88
00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.561
But it looks great on paper that it's theoretically available, but it's technically

89
00:06:28.619 --> 00:06:32.910
not that easy. And the Reserve Bank have come up with a pricing model which

90
00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.440
puts a fixed rate price before you even start.

91
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.686
And that has effectively meant that most of the NBDTs have decided it's

92
00:06:41.686 --> 00:06:44.880
commercially not viable for them to be involved.

93
00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:49.335
So fixed rate price, and that fixed rate price doesn't look very high, but that's

94
00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:54.720
an example where for small organizations, every bit of this cost actually ends up

95
00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.470
being disproportional. Another example is confirmation of payee.

96
00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:04.081
Great initiative by the bank, through the banking association to set up extra

97
00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:08.699
benefits and fraud protection for people making payments.

98
00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:12.660
This was implemented without involvement of the smaller organizations that

99
00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.820
effectively do exactly the same thing.

100
00:07:15.331 --> 00:07:20.790
We've now got involved, but once we got involved, the pricing model for

101
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.530
confirmation of payee has a fixed rate price and a variable rate price, again,

102
00:07:25.591 --> 00:07:29.610
making it largely uneconomic. Good news for us is that we've been able to negotiate

103
00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.490
a better price, and we now have confirmation of payee.

104
00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:36.466
But again, it's just there's still a fixed rate price in there, which is

105
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.760
disproportional for smaller players.

106
00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.811
And then, of course, Brent's related to the prudential levy as well.

107
00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:50.701
The Reserve Bank's preferred approach is to have a fixed rate fee and then a

108
00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:55.740
variable on top. Once again, what you're seeing is any one of these things alone is

109
00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.670
probably okay, but as you add all of these different layers up, you're adding a

110
00:07:59.701 --> 00:08:03.960
fixed rate cost to the participation of small organizations.

111
00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.370
I'm just conscious about time, so I've actually just...

112
00:08:08.401 --> 00:08:13.710
So I suppose the key thing for us is that we need to ensure that embedded, what we

113
00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:18.375
almost say would be anti-competitive pricing, effectively if we find a way to sort

114
00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:22.725
of make sure that we can make sure that for organizations of a smaller scale who

115
00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.760
play a really important role in New Zealand, have still got the ability to provide

116
00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:31.050
those services for their customers and their members. I'll pass on to Emma.

117
00:08:32.401 --> 00:08:33.211
Thanks, Kevin, and-

118
00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.091
We've only got five minutes left too, so if you want some questions.

119
00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:41.220
Yes. I'll keep it very brief. So I think I just want to round it out by

120
00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.745
saying, what Kevin and what Brent are saying is that

121
00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.725
there's been a lot of really good stuff happening, but the key is whether it works

122
00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:54.840
in practice. So we do want to really acknowledge that real progress has been made,

123
00:08:54.931 --> 00:08:59.640
the ESAS, the proportionality framework, capital settings, and use of the word

124
00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:05.280
bank. Those are big deals for the NBDTs, and they will make a

125
00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.270
difference.

126
00:09:07.872 --> 00:09:12.042
But what we really need, and the question that you asked us was, what settings do

127
00:09:12.102 --> 00:09:16.932
we want to see? We're really pleased with all the settings at a headline level, but

128
00:09:16.992 --> 00:09:20.831
the detail needs to be continually observed.

129
00:09:21.762 --> 00:09:25.992
And so that's our ask of the committee, is to see, so when these things are being

130
00:09:26.383 --> 00:09:31.572
implemented, what is happening in practice? Are people using them?

131
00:09:31.633 --> 00:09:34.452
Is it making the change that we want to see?

132
00:09:35.922 --> 00:09:39.762
Because there will need to be constant monitoring and recalibration to make sure

133
00:09:39.822 --> 00:09:45.207
that the detail, which is sometimes a little bit harder to keep interest in, but

134
00:09:45.222 --> 00:09:47.682
that is really where the rubber hits the road.

135
00:09:48.192 --> 00:09:52.782
So we'll leave it there, and please ask us any questions.

136
00:09:53.877 --> 00:09:56.261
Thank you. We'll go first to Aradhana Williams.

137
00:09:56.952 --> 00:10:00.672
Thank you. We don't have a lot of time, so forgive me for short questions.

138
00:10:01.542 --> 00:10:07.392
First, to your point, Kevin, how much have you

139
00:10:07.422 --> 00:10:10.992
been charged to access ECAS? We need some specifics about that because the

140
00:10:11.022 --> 00:10:16.722
government accepted EPC's recommendation that the regulatory settings for non-bank

141
00:10:16.752 --> 00:10:20.352
deposit takers should be a focus, and everyone's on board.

142
00:10:21.462 --> 00:10:27.432
Yeah. So it's a $8,000 application fee, and it's $2,000 a month to participate.

143
00:10:28.092 --> 00:10:28.332
Okay.

144
00:10:28.767 --> 00:10:32.292
And again, that doesn't sound like a lot of money, but again, it's the cumulative

145
00:10:32.322 --> 00:10:33.432
effect of some of these things.

146
00:10:34.032 --> 00:10:37.842
And it's the initial setup cost that is, in your view, more problematic than the

147
00:10:37.902 --> 00:10:38.457
ongoing cost?

148
00:10:38.892 --> 00:10:42.657
I think the ongoing 2,000, because effectively we would only be using it a few

149
00:10:42.672 --> 00:10:47.488
times a week, so if you work out a proportional per transaction cost, it's actually

150
00:10:47.532 --> 00:10:47.922
quite high-

151
00:10:48.597 --> 00:10:48.597
Yeah

152
00:10:48.597 --> 00:10:51.252
... for someone like us compared to a second ANZ, for example.

153
00:10:52.062 --> 00:10:54.372
So that's where it becomes problematic.

154
00:10:55.363 --> 00:10:57.762
Understood. Okay. More general question.

155
00:10:58.633 --> 00:11:04.542
There are still only 15 licensed non-bank deposit takers, I think, but one

156
00:11:04.602 --> 00:11:09.522
new entrant in July. Are you seeing more interest because of those three changes

157
00:11:09.672 --> 00:11:12.162
that the sector's been advocating for a long time?

158
00:11:12.883 --> 00:11:13.377
Emma,

159
00:11:14.832 --> 00:11:15.342
your question.

160
00:11:15.492 --> 00:11:18.492
Yeah. Definitely seeing more interest in the last

161
00:11:20.742 --> 00:11:23.637
couple of years since the Deposit Takers Act has gone through, and the use of the

162
00:11:23.682 --> 00:11:28.107
word bank has really escalated interest, and we're seeing people from overseas and

163
00:11:28.152 --> 00:11:31.092
people within New Zealand looking now to enter the market.

164
00:11:31.122 --> 00:11:32.562
It's just it will take time.

165
00:11:33.192 --> 00:11:36.642
Okay. So you're seeing a few of those new entrants, but we're not seeing that yet,

166
00:11:36.702 --> 00:11:41.787
and a growth of about, I think 700 million in the sector, but you're still 0.4% of

167
00:11:41.802 --> 00:11:46.032
total lending in New Zealand. What's it going to take to grow?

168
00:11:46.302 --> 00:11:51.613
Do you think anything you've asked for today is the ticket?

169
00:11:52.152 --> 00:11:56.502
If I may just jump in there. The real key is, if you're a lender, how do you raise

170
00:11:56.532 --> 00:11:59.742
your capital? So do you raise it from another bank?

171
00:11:59.802 --> 00:12:04.363
And so you'll know that Westpac are a very significant lender to intermediaries.

172
00:12:04.872 --> 00:12:05.052
So

173
00:12:06.912 --> 00:12:10.812
intermediaries get their facility from Westpac, then they re-lend.

174
00:12:11.202 --> 00:12:15.912
So we're a non-bank deposit taker, so we actually take deposits from the public,

175
00:12:16.302 --> 00:12:20.922
whereas other people raise it through systems like unit trusts, et cetera.

176
00:12:21.071 --> 00:12:27.027
So I think that is good, healthy competition between all that sector to create that

177
00:12:27.102 --> 00:12:32.113
tension. So it will keep going up, but it's still a limit on

178
00:12:32.772 --> 00:12:36.462
what this sector will be able to raise, how much capital this sector will be able

179
00:12:36.492 --> 00:12:36.942
to raise.

180
00:12:38.052 --> 00:12:39.912
Thanks. David Wilson.

181
00:12:40.002 --> 00:12:45.552
Yeah. Do you think there's any reason why would the world end with banks if they

182
00:12:45.613 --> 00:12:49.483
were compelled to use fixed rates that are issued by the RBNZ?

183
00:12:54.012 --> 00:12:59.846
I think it would get very rigid, very regimented, and

184
00:13:00.012 --> 00:13:05.067
I don't think you'd get the market reactions that allows that

185
00:13:05.113 --> 00:13:10.782
flexibility for us to say, for example, do we want to lend money on AI?

186
00:13:10.873 --> 00:13:13.152
Do we want to lend it for systems on AI?

187
00:13:13.782 --> 00:13:18.792
So would the Reserve Bank or somebody else be a party who would price that?

188
00:13:18.852 --> 00:13:20.922
I'd be very reluctant to see that occur.

189
00:13:21.702 --> 00:13:24.072
Quick question from me, just on switching.

190
00:13:24.553 --> 00:13:28.392
What would make it easier for customers to switch their deposit savings and lending

191
00:13:28.422 --> 00:13:29.712
from major banks to

192
00:13:34.422 --> 00:13:40.182
Sorry if I'm dominating this, but the key on this is when your deposit comes up for

193
00:13:40.212 --> 00:13:43.452
maturity, you're looking around to see who will I invest with?

194
00:13:43.887 --> 00:13:48.252
And you'll look at the interest rate, and you'll just go to one of those other

195
00:13:48.312 --> 00:13:53.983
banks and have a look. And secondly, you'll see what term do I want?

196
00:13:54.373 --> 00:13:59.322
So what advice do I need to say, "Hey, put this away for one month, or one year, or

197
00:14:00.042 --> 00:14:01.692
five years," or whatever it might be.

198
00:14:02.113 --> 00:14:05.053
Yeah, that's on the deposit side. What about the lending side in terms of the

199
00:14:05.113 --> 00:14:05.892
mortgage markets?

200
00:14:06.942 --> 00:14:12.762
Because we have fixed rate deposits, then we have to have fixed rate

201
00:14:13.032 --> 00:14:18.553
loans as well. So to get the matching of that, we match exactly on that with a

202
00:14:18.583 --> 00:14:24.012
slight skew to the front end. So that stops that sudden rush on your deposits.

203
00:14:24.733 --> 00:14:29.277
Can I just add to that as well? One of the good things that is coming in from 1st

204
00:14:29.412 --> 00:14:33.373
October obviously is the new risk weight settings under the capital settings, and

205
00:14:33.402 --> 00:14:35.863
that's obviously been part of the influence of this committee as well.

206
00:14:36.327 --> 00:14:39.912
So it's going to be really interesting to see how now we have to have alignment of

207
00:14:39.926 --> 00:14:45.792
risk weights and, I suppose, an equal playing field with the main banks for

208
00:14:45.852 --> 00:14:50.458
how each asset is rated. That's going to provide an opportunity for us to be able

209
00:14:50.592 --> 00:14:55.062
to be more competitive, especially in light housing, and obviously now we've got

210
00:14:55.123 --> 00:14:58.452
the introduction of new risk weights for small business lending as well.

211
00:14:58.962 --> 00:15:03.777
That's going to effectively be a positive thing for our sector about us being able

212
00:15:03.792 --> 00:15:05.983
to compete more and offer a more competitive product.

213
00:15:06.733 --> 00:15:08.172
Yeah. Look, we're going to have to end it there.

214
00:15:08.233 --> 00:15:10.212
Thank you so much, Kevin, Emma, and Brent.

215
00:15:10.272 --> 00:15:14.202
Really appreciate your contribution to the third round of the inquiry.

216
00:15:14.217 --> 00:15:15.162
Thank you.

217
00:15:15.252 --> 00:15:18.132
Thank you too.

218
00:15:18.222 --> 00:15:20.053
All right. We've got Revolut in the room.

219
00:15:21.342 --> 00:15:23.053
Welcome, Ryan and Georgia.

220
00:15:28.123 --> 00:15:28.692
No kidding.

221
00:15:33.613 --> 00:15:34.932
Thank you both for joining us.

222
00:15:35.157 --> 00:15:35.157
Thank you.

223
00:15:35.172 --> 00:15:37.182
We have received your written submission.

224
00:15:37.422 --> 00:15:39.492
If you have got some opening comments, you're welcome to them.

225
00:15:39.822 --> 00:15:42.072
Please keep them short because I know there's some good questions already

226
00:15:42.102 --> 00:15:43.392
circulating amongst committee.

227
00:15:44.202 --> 00:15:44.863
Excellent.

228
00:15:47.382 --> 00:15:50.083
Well, good morning, Chair and committee members.

229
00:15:51.402 --> 00:15:55.977
Thank you for the opportunity to speak today.

